1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Rio de Janeiro. The evening of May thirty-first, 2009. Air France 447 leaves for Paris with 228 people aboard.

2
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Three hours out over the Atlantic, the crew reports its position. Soon after, the aircraft passes the edge of radar coverage.

3
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:56,500
Ahead is the band of storms that circles the equator. The captain goes to rest. Two copilots now have the controls.

4
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:08,500
The flying copilot warns the cabin crew. More turbulence is coming.

5
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:19,500
They turn left, probably to avoid weather on the radar.

6
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:39,500
Ten minutes past two. The autopilot disconnects. Ice crystals have most likely blocked the speed sensors, and the speed readings no longer agree.

7
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:57,500
The flight-control mode changes, and the automatic protection against a stall is gone. The flying copilot pulls the nose up.

8
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:08,500
The aircraft climbs at nearly 7,000 feet a minute.

9
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:27,500
Within half a minute, the left speed display is reliable again. Nobody calls for the procedure written for lost speed readings.

10
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:38,500
The stall warning sounds continuously now. The nose stays up.

11
00:02:39,000 --> 00:03:00,500
At about 38,000 feet the climb ends. The wings can no longer provide enough lift, and the aircraft begins to descend with its nose still raised.

12
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:15,500
The captain returns to the cockpit. The crew does not agree on what is happening.

13
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:36,500
Then the stall warning goes silent, because the system rejects its readings when measured speed falls too low. The silence does not mean recovery.

14
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:54,500
Ten thousand feet. Both copilots are now moving their controls at once, one mostly nose down, the other mostly alternating.

15
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:05,500
About 2,000 feet above the sea, the ground warnings begin.

16
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:23,500
The recording ends. The aircraft struck the ocean in one piece, nose up, falling at nearly 11,000 feet a minute.

17
00:04:24,000 --> 00:05:08,500
It took about two years to find the wreckage, 3,900 meters down. The recorders helped investigators trace confusion, an unrecognized stall, and the likely icing of speed sensors. Speed probes were replaced, and pilot training changed. In 2026, a Paris appeal court convicted Airbus and Air France of involuntary homicide. Airbus has said it will seek a further review.

18
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:37,500
Kuala Lumpur, about forty minutes after midnight on March eighth, 2014. Malaysia Airlines 370 departs for Beijing with 239 people aboard.

19
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:49,500
The crew reports cruising at 35,000 feet. No problem is announced.

20
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:12,500
Controllers hand the flight over to Ho Chi Minh. The captain's reply is the last recorded voice transmission from this aircraft. "Good night, Malaysian Three Seven Zero."

21
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:33,500
Just over a minute later, near a waypoint called Igari, the last position broadcast goes out. Its symbol soon disappears from the controllers' screens.

22
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:50,500
But military radar is still watching. The aircraft has turned back across Malaysia, with no further recorded voice transmission.

23
00:06:51,000 --> 00:07:06,500
Near Penang, the first officer's phone registers with the network. There is no call and no message.

24
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:22,500
Northwest of Sumatra, military radar sees it one last time. This is the last position that exists.

25
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:41,500
Three minutes later, the satellite terminal logs back on, probably after a power interruption. What interrupted the satellite link is unknown.

26
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:55,500
The airline calls the aircraft by satellite. The equipment responds. No one picks up.

27
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:29,500
From here, there is no picture of this flight. There is only a brief automatic signal, about once an hour, which places the aircraft somewhere on a curve. The path you are watching is one published model of where it went, among many.

28
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:43,500
On the ground, Kuala Lumpur activates its rescue center, hours after the last contact.

29
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:51,500
A second satellite call goes unanswered.

30
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:06,500
Dawn is reaching the southern Indian Ocean. The aircraft answers one more routine satellite check.

31
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:30,500
Eight minutes later, it asks to reconnect. Those last transmissions suggest a rapid descent. They do not say when or where it ended. After this, there is nothing.

32
00:09:31,000 --> 00:10:20,500
In 2015, a wing part washed up on Réunion Island. More debris followed, on shores across the western Indian Ocean. The first seabed search covered over 120,000 square kilometers. Another surveyed over 112,000, partly overlapping it, and further surveys continued in 2026. They have not found the aircraft. Investigators say the turn back was likely made by hand. Who did it, and why, is not known.

33
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:51,500
New York, a summer evening, July seventeenth, 1996. After a delay at the gate, TWA 800 lifts off for Paris with 230 people aboard.

34
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:10,500
The sun has just set. Climbing along the coast of Long Island, the crew is told to hold at 13,000 feet.

35
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:32,500
The captain notices something on the panel. "Look at that crazy fuel flow indicator there on number four." No link to what follows was ever established.

36
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:47,500
Controllers clear a climb to 15,000 feet. Power comes up. It is the last radio exchange.

37
00:11:48,000 --> 00:12:00,500
Twelve seconds past 8:31. A brief, loud sound, and the recorders stop.

38
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:27,500
The explosion began in the fuel tank between the wings. Within about five seconds, the nose of the aircraft separated. Everything you see from here is from models, because no recording exists.

39
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:48,500
Without its nose, the rest of the aircraft kept flying. Models show it climbing for a short time, to perhaps 15,000 or 16,000 feet.

40
00:12:49,000 --> 00:13:05,500
About half a minute after the explosion, the outer wings failed. Burning fuel lit the sky above the water.

41
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:27,500
Hundreds of people saw it from the shore, from boats and from the air. A nearby airline captain radioed that he had seen an explosion.

42
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:44,500
The main wreckage hit the ocean less than a minute after the explosion. All 230 people aboard died.

43
00:13:45,000 --> 00:14:33,500
Some witnesses described a streak of light rising, and many people came to believe a missile had been fired. The FBI found no evidence of a crime. The NTSB, working from wreckage rebuilt in a hangar, concluded the tank exploded, probably from excess voltage in fuel gauge wiring, though the source was never found. Petitioners challenged that in 2013. The Board denied the petition.

44
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:44,500
New rules now reduce flammable vapor in fuel tanks.

45
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:18,500
New York, 1:20 in the morning, October thirty-first, 1999. EgyptAir 990 leaves for Cairo with 217 people aboard. Its crew includes a relief first officer, for the long crossing.

46
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:35,500
It levels off at cruising height, over the dark Atlantic. The relief first officer has taken the right seat.

47
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:49,500
The captain says he will be away for a moment, and leaves the cockpit.

48
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:06,500
The autopilot disengages. No warning sounds. The relief first officer repeats a quiet phrase. "I rely on God."

49
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:23,500
The engine power levers move to idle. Both tail controls move down, and the nose starts to drop.

50
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:36,500
The captain's voice is recorded as he comes back. "What's happening? What's happening?"

51
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:58,500
The nose is about 40 degrees below level. The aircraft is falling at an estimated 39,000 feet a minute, and nearing the speed of sound.

52
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:25,500
Now the two tail controls split. The left moves to raise the nose, the right to push it down. Fuel to both engines is cut off. Who moved those controls is not recorded.

53
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,500
The captain calls out. "Pull with me."

54
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:47,500
With the engines stopped, the recorders lose power. From here, only radar remains.

55
00:17:48,000 --> 00:18:02,500
Radar suggests the aircraft climbed back to about 25,000 feet and turned. Then it descended again.

56
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:15,500
It struck the ocean at about this time. All 217 people aboard died.

57
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:56,500
The two countries read the same recorders and reached different conclusions. The United States blamed the relief first officer's control movements, and did not determine why he made them. Egypt rejected a deliberate act, left the cause unresolved, and considered an elevator defect likely. The words on the recording do not settle it.

58
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:34,500
Auckland, the morning of November twenty-eighth, 1979. 257 people board a sightseeing flight to Antarctica and back. Before it leaves, the last waypoint in its route is moved east. The crew are not told.

59
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:56,500
Five hours later, near McMurdo, a forecaster reports low cloud around 2,000 feet. The captain plans to go lower, to show the passengers the ice.

60
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:10,500
The captain sees a break in the cloud, and circles down through it, twice.

61
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:31,500
The autopilot picks up the programmed route again. Because of the changed waypoint, that line now leads to the northern slopes of Mount Erebus.

62
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:52,500
They settle at 1,500 feet. White ground, white cloud. In this light, a rising snow slope and a far horizon can look the same.

63
00:20:53,000 --> 00:21:08,500
The captain decides to leave. "We're twenty six miles north. We'll have to climb out of this."

64
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:19,500
They cross the coast. The ground starts to rise.

65
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:31,500
Six and a half seconds before impact, the ground warning sounds.

66
00:21:32,000 --> 00:22:09,500
All 257 aboard died. The first investigation mainly blamed the decision to fly low. A Royal Commission then blamed the route change and the airline's procedures, and cleared the crew. The airline apologized for its treatment of families in 2009. In 2019, the government and the airline apologized again.

67
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:50,500
Tokyo, August twelfth, 1985. Japan Air Lines 123 takes off for Osaka with 524 people. Seven years earlier, this aircraft struck its tail on landing. The repair to its rear pressure wall was done incorrectly, and inspectors missed it.

68
00:22:51,000 --> 00:23:18,500
Twelve minutes in, at 24,000 feet, a loud sound. The pressure wall has failed. Air bursts into the tail, and tears away much of the fin and the hydraulic power that moves the controls.

69
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:27,500
The captain asks to return to Tokyo.

70
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:50,500
The aircraft begins to climb and fall, and to sway from side to side. The controls cannot stop it. In the cabin, the oxygen masks are down.

71
00:23:51,000 --> 00:24:01,500
The captain tells controllers the aircraft cannot be controlled.

72
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:16,500
The crew has little left to work with but engine power. The landing gear comes down.

73
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:31,500
The sun has set. Power comes back, and the aircraft descends faster, over the mountains.

74
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:40,500
Speed falls as low as 108 knots.

75
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:57,500
The crew asks where they are. Controllers tell them Tokyo and the American base at Yokota are both ready.

76
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:06,500
The nose drops into a steep descent.

77
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:25,500
A ground warning. Engine power comes up, and the descent briefly stops. Then the right wing strikes trees on a ridge.

78
00:25:26,000 --> 00:26:11,500
It stayed in the air for 32 minutes after the failure. 520 people died, the most ever in a single aircraft accident. Rescuers did not reach the site until the next morning, and found four survivors, all seated in the rear of the cabin. Afterward, the 747 tail and hydraulic systems were changed to protect against a failure like this.

79
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:44,500
Buenos Aires, August second, 1947. The airliner Star Dust leaves for Santiago, with six passengers and five crew. Forecasts warn of snowstorms and cloud over the Andes.

80
00:26:45,000 --> 00:27:06,500
The crew reports its position by Morse code, flying at 10,000 feet. There is no radar, and no recorder. These reports are nearly all that survive.

81
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:19,500
Near Mendoza, they report climbing toward 24,000 feet to cross the mountains.

82
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:35,500
They revise their arrival to 17:45. Investigators later inferred they were in cloud. No position was reported.

83
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:57,500
Four minutes before they expect to land, the Santiago operator receives the last message. It ends with one word. S, T, E, N, D, E, C.

84
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:22,500
Reception is strong and clear, but fast. The operator asks them to repeat it. The aircraft sends the same word twice more. No one has ever established what it means.

85
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:42,500
Star Dust was still in cloud when it struck Mount Tupungato. All eleven aboard died. They were not where they thought they were.

86
00:28:43,000 --> 00:29:23,500
Searches found nothing, and the aircraft became a legend. In 1998, climbers found an engine on the glacier. The ice had carried the wreckage downhill, for more than fifty years. Argentine investigators concluded an incorrect speed calculation and bad weather led the crew to descend too early. The final word is still unexplained.

87
00:29:34,000 --> 00:30:06,500
Lae, New Guinea, July second, 1937. Amelia Earhart and her navigator, Fred Noonan, take off for Howland Island, a strip of land about two kilometers long, thousands of kilometers away. Only a few legs of a flight around the world remain.

88
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:19,500
Seven hours out, Lae hears its last report. They are above the clouds.

89
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:39,500
Through the night, the Coast Guard ship Itasca waits at Howland. Its radio picks up a voice through static, too faint to use.

90
00:30:40,000 --> 00:31:05,500
Morning. Earhart asks Itasca for a bearing, and estimates she is 200 miles out. About half an hour later, she says 100 miles. Itasca cannot get a direction on that frequency.

91
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:20,500
She reports flying at 1,000 feet, and low on fuel. She cannot see the ship.

92
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:42,500
She hears the ship's signals now, but cannot use them to find its direction. The ship is sending smoke, and calling. Neither can find the other.

93
00:31:43,000 --> 00:32:06,500
Her last clear message is entered in the log. "We are on the line 157 337." She gives no point of reference. Then nothing that can be understood.

94
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:56,500
The Navy search found no trace and concluded they were lost at sea. Others believe that line led them to Nikumaroro, 650 kilometers to the southeast, where bones were found in 1940, then lost. Neither idea has been proven. A sonar image in 2024 proved to be rock. A lagoon target in 2026 was judged likely to be tree debris. The aircraft has never been found.

95
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:32,500
Libya, April fourth, 1943. A new American bomber called Lady Be Good takes off on its first mission, to bomb Naples. Nine men are aboard. Blowing sand breaks up the formation.

96
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:48,500
Alone, the crew turns for home after dark, and releases its bombs. Where they fell isn't known.

97
00:33:49,000 --> 00:34:11,500
The crew reports its direction finder isn't working, and asks for a radio bearing. Later investigators suggested they mistook which side of the station they were on.

98
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:38,500
Around midnight, men near the base hear a bomber pass overhead, and fire flares. It was probably this one. Investigators believed they flew on into the Sahara, mistaking the desert for the sea.

99
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:47,500
Two hours later, the fuel runs out.

100
00:34:48,000 --> 00:35:04,500
They jump into the dark and land in desert, not water. Eight of the nine find each other.

101
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:18,500
The empty bomber flies on, and comes down on a gravel plain, largely intact.

102
00:35:19,000 --> 00:36:11,500
They may have thought the coast was close. It was hundreds of kilometers away. They walked north with half a canteen of water, sharing it by the capful, and left arrows made of parachutes behind them. Diaries record the days. Five stopped at the dunes. Three went on, for help. Oil surveyors found the bomber in 1958. Later searches found the crew's trail, and remains and diaries followed in 1960.

103
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:45,500
Fort Lauderdale, Florida, December fifth, 1945. Five Navy Avengers leave for a routine training flight, with fourteen men aboard. Their route is a triangle out over the Bahamas.

104
00:36:46,000 --> 00:37:01,500
After the turn, another instructor overhears them. They are talking about their compasses, and about being lost.

105
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:29,500
The flight leader reports both his compasses out. He believes he is over the Florida Keys. If so, north would lead home. If he is really over the Bahamas, north leads out to sea.

106
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:41,500
He passes a small island, with no other land in sight.

107
00:37:42,000 --> 00:38:01,500
They try northeast, then east, then west. He says they will fly west until they reach land, or run out of fuel.

108
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:14,500
The sun sets at Fort Lauderdale. Weather over the sea is getting worse.

109
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:38,500
Radio stations finally place them within about 100 miles of a point off northern Florida. The estimate reaches the base, but it is never broadcast to the flight.

110
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:53,500
Earlier, a warning was relayed that they might soon have to land on the water.

111
00:38:54,000 --> 00:39:13,500
Just after seven in the evening, one pilot calls the leader. There is no answer. It is the last call anyone logged.

112
00:39:14,000 --> 00:40:02,500
A Mariner search plane took off with thirteen men. Twenty-three minutes later, a passing tanker saw flames on the sea. The Navy first blamed the flight leader, then withdrew that, and recorded the cause as unknown. No trace of any of the six aircraft has been confirmed. The Bermuda Triangle story came later. The records describe a lost formation, rough seas, and radio trouble.

